In Unity, if you want to prevent a `GameObject` from receiving touch events, you can achieve this through several methods. Here are some common approaches:

### 1. Use a Raycast

When you're handling touch events with a raycast, you can conditionally ignore certain objects. For example:

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class TouchHandler : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Update()
    {
        // Check for touch input
        if (Input.touchCount > 0)
        {
            Touch touch = Input.GetTouch(0);
            RaycastHit hit;
            Ray ray = Camera.main.ScreenPointToRay(touch.position);

            if (Physics.Raycast(ray, out hit))
            {
                // Check if the touched object is not the one you want to ignore
                if (hit.transform.gameObject.CompareTag("Interactable"))
                {
                    // Handle touch event for interactable objects
                    Debug.Log("Touched: " + hit.transform.name);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Make sure to assign the proper tag to the objects you want to be able to interact with.

### 2. Ignore Touch Events in the Script

If you have a script attached to a GameObject and you want that GameObject to ignore touch events, you can handle the touch events in a centralized manner (e.g., in a game manager) and simply not process them for that GameObject. 

For example:

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class IgnoreTouch : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Update()
    {
        // Check for touch input
        if (Input.touchCount > 0)
        {
            // Ignore touch events here
            return;
        }
    }
}
```

### 3. Disable Raycast Target in UI

If you're dealing with UI elements and want to ignore touch events, you can set the `raycastTarget` property of `Image` or `Button` components to `false`:

```csharp
using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.UI;

public class IgnoreTouchUI : MonoBehaviour
{
    public Image image;

    void Start()
    {
        if (image != null)
        {
            image.raycastTarget = false; // Set to false to ignore touch events
        }
    }
}
```

### 4. Use a Custom Input Manager

You can create a custom input manager that selectively processes touch events based on certain criteria, for example:

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class InputManager : MonoBehaviour
{
    public GameObject ignoreObject; // Assign the object to ignore in the inspector

    void Update()
    {
        if (Input.touchCount > 0)
        {
            Touch touch = Input.GetTouch(0);
            // Check if the touch is on the ignored object
            if (touch.phase == TouchPhase.Began)
            {
                Ray ray = Camera.main.ScreenPointToRay(touch.position);
                if (Physics.Raycast(ray, out RaycastHit hit))
                {
                    if (hit.transform.gameObject != ignoreObject)
                    {
                        // Handle touch event for all objects except ignoreObject
                        Debug.Log("Touched: " + hit.transform.name);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Summary

Choose the method that seems most appropriate for your project's architecture and needs. The key idea behind ignoring touch events is to manage how and when they get processed based on your game's logic.

